inflation's at a 40-year high and lots  of people are wondering what the hell's  going on in bitcoin bitcoin's price has  come down 57 from its all-time high but  one of the beauties of the bitcoin  market is that we have on-chain metrics  we can literally go look right on a  blockchain and understand who's buying  who's selling who paid what for their  bitcoin and how long have they been  holding it one of the metrics that i  continue to pay attention to is this one  right here what it shows is how much of  bitcoin circulating supply has not moved  in at least one year and what you can  see is that blue line continues to tick  upwards it's sitting right now at an  all-time high 65.5  of all bitcoin in circulation has not  moved in at least a year even though the  price has gone up the price has gone  down inflation has gone up and there's  been all sorts of geopolitical risk in  the world  supply chains maybe get disrupted and  lots of investors are speculating in  markets but 65.5 of bitcoin has not  moved over the last 12 months those are  the long-term holders those are the  strong hands they bought bitcoin they  understand bitcoin and they realize that  is probably one of the most certain  things in an uncertain world if you  listen to someone like michael saylor i  think that looking at these metrics  continues to tell a story that's very  different than the public narrative  65.5 of all bitcoin not moving in a year  means not only is the finite asset of 21  million bitcoin actually more illiquid  than most people think because the 19  million bitcoin or so that are in  circulation  65.5 percent aren't moving that leaves  only about 35 or so available for people  to buy and sell new demand to soak up  and so of course there hasn't been any  sort of strong catalyst in the last  couple of months but if you have a  highly illiquid market and there is some  catalyst some change in demand in the  future it likely will lead to an  explosive move upwards in price now i  don't know when that happens i don't  know what the catalyst will be but this  is a very similar market dynamic to what  we saw going all the way back to q3 q4  of 2020.  at that point the market got to a very  high level of illiquidity and of course  we had people arguing that gbtc trade  inflation was on the horizon and as soon  as paul tudor jones and many other  people started to say that they were  buying bitcoin lots and lots of new  demand showed up you also had the  bitcoin having in may of 2020 all  drivers of that new demand and when that  occurred bitcoin went from about 10 000  all the way up to sixty four thousand  dollars in march of 2021. now i'm not  saying that we're gonna get the same  severity of a move but what i am saying  is that the market structure the  illiquidity that you can see and measure  based on these on-chain metrics it's a  repeat of what we've seen in the past if  there is a catalyst there is an increase  in demand it should lead to something  that we've seen before as well which is  a very rapid increase in price now the  us dollar price is the only thing that  can move you have a fixed supply asset  and you have an increase in demand in  order to accommodate everyone the price  goes up be interesting to watch this if  the illiquidity persists at some point  we'll get that catalyst and then it'll  be the fireworks that many of the  bitcoin community are waiting for
 

